WEBVTT

00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:02.973
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> <i>The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind</i>

00:00:03.592 --> 00:00:04.500
Part 1

00:00:13.118 --> 00:00:16.263
<v><b>William:</b></v> …Standing on the porch one morning during a heavy rain,

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:21.791
I watched a line of people walking slowly past like a great army of ants.

00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.501
They were our neighbours and farmers from other villages.

00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:30.518
The women carried basins on their heads containing all their pots and pans,

00:00:30.698 --> 00:00:33.746
water buckets, even bundles of clothing.

00:00:34.278 --> 00:00:38.259
They stopped to rest every few metres, exhausted from hunger,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.070
before hoisting their heavy loads and continuing on…

00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:50.800
On the first day of February, the head teacher made this announcement at assembly.

00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:57.996
‘We’re all aware of the problems across the country, which we also face,’ he said.

00:00:58.617 --> 00:01:02.340
‘But many of you still haven’t paid your school fees for this term.

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:05.962
Starting tomorrow, the grace period is over.’

00:01:06.756 --> 00:01:13.000
My hungry stomach tied itself into another knot because I knew my father hadn’t paid my fees

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.901
– 1,200 kwacha, collected three times a year.

00:01:19.195 --> 00:01:22.810
When I got home, I found my father in the fields.

00:01:23.202 --> 00:01:30.049
‘They’re saying I should bring my fees tomorrow, 1,200 kwacha,’ I said.

00:01:30.769 --> 00:01:33.905
‘So we should pay them. They aren’t joking.’

00:01:35.092 --> 00:01:37.573
My father gave me the look I’d grown to fear.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.103
‘You know our problems here, son,’ he said. ‘We have nothing.’

00:01:44.138 --> 00:01:45.731
I missed school terribly.

00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:49.412
I remembered that the previous year a group called

00:01:49.438 --> 00:01:53.581
the Malawi Teacher Training Activity had opened a small library…

00:01:53.895 --> 00:01:57.325
that was stocked with books donated by the American government.

00:01:58.364 --> 00:02:02.510
Perhaps reading could keep my brain from getting soft while being a dropout.

00:02:03.474 --> 00:02:08.611
One day, I noticed a book pushed into the shelf and slightly concealed.

00:02:09.296 --> 00:02:13.325
It was an American textbook called <i>Using Energy</i>.

00:02:14.555 --> 00:02:17.508
The cover featured a long row of windmills

00:02:17.808 --> 00:02:21.831
– though at the time I had no idea what a windmill was...

00:02:24.521 --> 00:02:29.541
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> William was fascinated by the giant machines that towered into the sky,

00:02:30.164 --> 00:02:36.843
and when he discovered they could make electricity from the wind, he decided to try to build one.